{
  "evidence": "- TEXT_D: Maintain a comprehensive NHS free at point of need; extra annual NHS funding; maximum six‑month wait from diagnosis to treatment; restore free eye/dental checks; freeze prescription charges. End rough sleeping by 2000; home‑insulation programme prioritising low‑income households; Carer’s Benefit; childcare strategy; stronger victim support. Extensive equality measures: ECHR into UK law; Human Rights Commission; civil rights for disabled; equal age of consent; ban discrimination in housing/employment (incl. armed forces); repeal Section 28; enforce equal pay; protections for ethnic minorities; women’s rights; parental leave. Respect for institutions/authority and national security: retain Trident (reduced warheads); support NATO; constitutional reforms and devolution via referendums and lawful processes.\n- TEXT_C: Bill of Rights with civil, social and economic rights (free healthcare, housing, education, decent work, minimum wage); integrate NHS; raise child benefit and pensions; end means‑testing for residential elderly care; free nursery education; restore student grants; build affordable homes. Law and order: community policing, “zero tolerance” initiatives; handgun ban. Loyalty/authority‑challenging changes: seek rapid Scottish independence; reform monarchy’s role; remove nuclear weapons; phased NATO withdrawal; create separate Scottish Defence Forces.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate profile prioritizes care and equality but also gives moderate weight to loyalty and authority. TEXT_D strongly advances care and equality while preserving core national institutions and procedures, whereas TEXT_C’s independence drive and NATO withdrawal conflict with the electorate’s moderate loyalty/authority concerns despite its robust social‑rights agenda.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
